issue_context.body:
**Context:**
- Playwright Version: v1.19.1
- Operating System: Linux
- Node.js version: v14.18
- Browser: Android APP

<!-- CLI to auto-capture this info -->
<!-- npx envinfo --preset playwright --markdown -->
## System:
 - OS: Linux 5.4 Ubuntu 20.04.3 LTS (Focal Fossa)
 - Memory: 196.99 MB / 15.45 GB
 - Container: Yes
## Binaries:
 - Node: 14.18.0 - /usr/local/bin/node
 - Yarn: 1.22.5 - /usr/bin/yarn
 - npm: 8.5.0 - /usr/local/bin/npm
## Languages:
 - Bash: 5.0.17 - /bin/bash

**Code Snippet**

```typescript
async () => {
    // Connect to the device.
    const [device] = await android.devices();
    console.log(`Model: ${device.model()}`);
    console.log(`Serial: ${device.serial()}`);

    // --------------------- WebView -----------------------

    // Launch an application with WebView.
    const app_package = 'com.my.app_package';
    await device.shell(`am force-stop ${app_package}`);
    await device.shell(`am start ${app_package}/.MainActivity`);
    // Get the WebView.
    const webview = await device.webView({ pkg: app_package }); // <- Error: androidWebView.page: tracesDir must be specified for BrowserContext

    // Work with WebView's page as usual.
    const page = await webview.page();
    console.log(await page.title()); 
}
```

**Describe the bug**

Well, we are working with the experimental Android feature. The aim is to test an Ionic APP (webview) and we are getting this error:

```
Error: androidWebView.page: tracesDir must be specified for BrowserContext
```

Following the trace we got into the `/node_modules/playwright-core/lib/server/android/android.js` file, line 285. It seems like a validation requires the tracesDir value filled in the BrowserOptions object, but in that file [(line 280)](https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/1215057ca1cf756f35e045a34fd340838bee81d8/packages/playwright-core/src/server/android/android.ts#L267) was setted an empty value hardcoded.

If we fill that variable with some value in that file (inside node_modules), even if the folder doesn't exist the tests are working properly. The APP is launched in the physical device and we can use the `page` object to interact with the webview page. But we don't find any way to pass it from outside as its value is hardcoded.

Is there anything we are missing?

Note: we are not using any kind of trace recording, at least to our knowledge hehe

Thank you so much.
